# {{ project_name }}

Built on **Terp** — secure-by-default by construction. The composition root is
`app/main.py`; the only editable surface is `app/modules/`.

## Setup

```bash
uv sync                               # backend deps (creates ./.venv and uv.lock)
{% if frontend %}npm --prefix frontend install         # frontend deps (creates frontend/package-lock.json)
{% endif %}{% if deploy_build == 'generated' %}uv run terp deployment render         # the production build files, from deployment.json
{% endif %}# who can reach which route, pinned: write it once and commit it
uv run terp inspect access --app app.main:build --format surface > authz-surface.json
uv run terp check                     # the architecture gate (== CI)
{% if frontend %}uv run terp dev                       # runs the API (:22100) + frontend (:21100) together
{% else %}uv run terp dev                       # runs the API (:22100); this app has no frontend of its own
{% endif %}```

Backend commands run through `uv run …` — the `terp` CLI and the project's Python
live in the local `.venv`, not on the global PATH.

**Commit `authz-surface.json`.** It records which role reaches which route (ADR 0209), and
`uv run terp verify` refuses a change to that the file does not record, naming each one as a
widening or a narrowing. Write the file again, with the command above, only once a person
has approved what changed, and in the same change, so the diff shows what they approved.

{% if frontend -%}
**Commit `uv.lock` and `frontend/package-lock.json`.** CI installs exactly them (`uv sync
--locked`, `npm ci`) and so do the production images, so what ships is what the gate
ran. After changing a dependency, `uv lock` (or `uv add`) and `npm --prefix frontend
install` bring the locks along; a lock left behind fails the build, and `uv run terp
verify --only locks` names the file and the command before it gets that far.

No starter module ships — sign-in, admin and audit are wired, and a fresh checkout is
gate-green as-is. `uv run terp new module <name>` (named after your actual domain) adds
the first module: model, migration, service, policy, router and a typed frontend view.

`uv run terp dev` refreshes the OpenAPI contract, then runs uvicorn and the Vite dev server
side by side, restarting the API when a Python source of the app changes; Ctrl+C stops
both. Both answer on the host ports claimed for this checkout (`uv run terp ports show`
prints them): the same pair a workbench and `docker compose` use, claimed on first run
past any port another application on this machine already holds. Prefer them apart? `uv run uvicorn app.main:app --reload` and `npm --prefix frontend
run dev` -- though on Windows uvicorn's own reloader stops restarting when it was not
started from an interactive terminal (an agent's shell, an editor task), which is why
`uv run terp dev` does the restarting itself. Without Docker the settings
default to an in-memory SQLite database, which the app can boot on but no migration can
act on — `uv run terp migrate upgrade` refuses it rather than report success against a
database that disappears with the process. So set `DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./dev.db` in
`.env` and run `uv run terp migrate upgrade` once (the Compose workbench below does all of
this with Postgres instead).
