{% if frontend %}
**If a frontend edit does not appear, restart the dev server rather than trusting HMR.**
Vite watches for file events, and some filesystems deliver them unreliably or not at all —
Docker Desktop bind mounts and volume-backed checkouts are the known cases, but a plain
Windows checkout on a synced or virtualised drive can drop them too. It fails *silently*:
the server keeps serving the last build it saw, so the browser shows stale output and
nothing reports an error. That is worth knowing before you conclude a change had no effect,
or screenshot a screen that no longer exists in the source.

Set `TERP_DEV_FORCE_POLLING=true` to make the watcher poll instead (300ms; `vite.config.ts`
reads it, and the Compose workbench sets it already). Polling costs some CPU and is the
right trade whenever the alternative is not noticing.

**The dev server refuses to be embedded in an iframe unless you say who may embed it.**
`vite.config.ts` serves the same origin rules as production, including
`frame-ancestors 'none'` — so a page that tries to frame the dev server gets a blank pane and
Chromium's "localhost refused to connect.", which describes a network failure this is not. If
you are viewing this app inside a workbench that previews it in a pane, that workbench sets
`TERP_DEV_FRAME_ANCESTORS` to its own browser origin (for example `http://localhost:8420`)
before starting the stack, and there is nothing for you to configure. Set it yourself only if
you are embedding the dev server in something of your own: one origin, no list and no `*`, and
anything malformed falls back to `'none'`. Production is unaffected — `nginx.conf` says
`frame-ancestors 'none'` with no knob.
{% endif %}
## Upgrading Terp

Terp releases in lockstep: every `terp-*` pin and every `@terpjs/*` dependency moves to the
same version together. Bump them all, then re-lock **with a fresh index read**:

```bash
uv sync --refresh                     # --refresh: see below
{% if frontend %}npm --prefix frontend install
{% endif %}uv run terp verify --profile full
```

{% if frontend %}Both installs rewrite a lock (`uv.lock`, `frontend/package-lock.json`); commit them with
the bumped pins.{% else %}The install rewrites `uv.lock`; commit it with the bumped pins.{% endif %}

Where `deployment.json` says `"build": "generated"`, the production build files
(`Dockerfile.prod`{% if frontend %}, `frontend/Dockerfile.prod`{% endif %}, `docker-compose.prod.yml`) are the
framework's: after the install, run `uv run terp deployment render` and commit what it
writes with the locks. A missed render turns `uv run terp verify --only generated` red.

A release publishes its distributions one at a time, so a cached index can hold a version
gap for minutes after the tag. uv reports that gap as `no version of terp-cap-<x>==<v>` —
indistinguishable from "this package was never released", and `uv run <anything>` re-resolves
first, so every command you reach for to diagnose it fails the same way with no output.
`--refresh` is the fix; suspect the cache before you suspect the release.

## Run with Docker

For a prod-parity stack (Postgres, not SQLite) that seeds itself and live-reloads on edits:

```bash
cp .env.example .env      # optional: set a strong SECRET_KEY
uv run terp docker dev    # (or: docker compose watch)
```

This builds the images, brings up Postgres, runs the migrations, seeds a usable admin
(`admin@example.test`, password `correct horse battery staple`), then serves
{% if frontend %}the API (`:22100`) and the frontend (`:21100`) — editing `app/` or `frontend/src/` live-syncs
into the running containers. A dependency change (`uv add`, `npm --prefix frontend install
<pkg>`) rebuilds its image and brings every service on it back up, and a saved `.app.env` or{% else %}the API (`:22100`) — editing `app/` live-syncs into the running container. A dependency
change (`uv add`) rebuilds the image and brings every service on it back up, and a saved `.app.env` or{% endif %}
`.env` is applied to the running stack by `uv run terp docker dev` itself
(`uv run terp guide environment`). The one-off `app/seed.py` is where you put demo data worth
working against; production bootstraps its first admin with
`uv run terp user create <email> --role admin` instead (never `terp seed`).

## Add a module

```bash
uv run terp new module billing        # scaffolds the five slots{% if frontend %} (+ a frontend slot){% endif %}
# mount it in app/main.py: import its `module`, add it to the modules list
uv run terp migrate make billing      # autogenerated migration
uv run terp check
```

See `AGENTS.md` and `uv run terp guide` for the authoring rules.

