## Frontend

A React frontend is wired in `frontend/` (Vite + `@terpjs/react-core`), with one module
slot matching the backend. `frontend/src/modules/<name>/module.tsx` is auto-discovered —
no central registration, and `uv run terp new module <name>` adds the matching slot for you.

`uv run terp dev` runs it next to the API (see Setup). To type calls to your own endpoints,
generate the typed client from your backend and pass its `paths` to `useTerpClient`:

```bash
uv run terp openapi                 # write openapi.json from the live app
uv run terp routes                  # write frontend/src/routes.gen.d.ts from the manifests
npm --prefix frontend run generate  # openapi.json -> frontend/src/api/schema.d.ts
```

Commit `openapi.json`. It is the app's API contract: the file a mobile app or a site in another
repository pins, and the one `uv run terp verify` holds to the API the app serves, naming every
change to its shape and which would break such a client (ADR 0194). Who may call a route is not
in it; authz-surface pins that. Regenerate it in the same change as the API it describes.

```ts
import type { paths } from "../../api/schema";
const client = useTerpClient<paths>();   // now typed to YOUR endpoints
```

## Conformance

End-to-end browser flows run with [Playwright](https://playwright.dev) in `conformance/`, using the
base-profile helpers from `@terpjs/conformance` (sign-in gating, sign-out). Boot the workbench, then:

```bash
npm --prefix conformance install
(cd conformance && npx playwright install --with-deps chromium)
uv run terp verify --profile release --only conformance   # drives the running stack
```

The suite needs to know where the stack answers, and it has no default: the workbench's host
ports are assigned per checkout (`uv run terp docker dev` assigns them, as does
`uv run terp ports assign`), so any fixed number would be some other checkout's. `terp verify`
reads this checkout's assignment and hands the address over. For a stack started another way,
name it yourself — `TERP_E2E_BASE_URL=http://localhost:21100 npm --prefix conformance test` for
`uv run terp dev` on its default port.

`conformance/tests/auth.spec.ts` covers the universal auth flow — add your own module specs
alongside it. It finds the sign-in screen and the shell by the framework's markers rather than
by their text, so it passes in whichever language this app ships; your own module specs are the
place for your own wording. CI runs the suite against the Docker workbench on every push.
{%- else -%}
## Clients

This app has no frontend of its own: a native app, a site built elsewhere or another service
is its client, and the API is the one surface they share. `uv run terp openapi` writes the
contract (`openapi.json`) every client types itself from, and `uv run terp verify` holds the
committed copy to the live app. A browser client on another origin needs its origin allowed in
`control_plane/__init__.py`, as `cors=CorsPolicy.allow(["https://app.your-domain.example"])` in
place of `CorsPolicy.disabled(...)` (never `*`, which production refuses); a native app or a
server needs nothing. `BACKEND_CORS_ORIGINS` allows nothing: it is read only to refuse `*`. In production the `api` unit is the deployment's only way in and publishes
`API_PORT` (23200 unless the deployment says otherwise). That port is uvicorn itself, which
neither terminates TLS nor times out a client that sends its request slowly, so production puts
a reverse proxy, a load balancer or an ingress in front of it (ADR 0202), counted in
`trusted_proxy_hops` (`control_plane/__init__.py`).
{%- endif %}
